uPQC SDK Developer Hub (https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvidia.github.io/cuPQC/releases/v0.6/\",\"pattern\":\"NTT\"}); Total lines: 795\nL472:   * 02\nL473: \nL474: Forward NTT\nL475: \nL476: Transform coefficient-domain vectors into the NTT domain.\nL477: \nL478: `execute()`standard, single kernel\nL479: \nL480: `stage_1_execute()``stage_2_execute()`staged, two passes\nL481: \nL482:   * 03\nL483: \nL484: Inverse NTT\nL485: \nL486: Transform NTT-domain vectors back to coefficient representation.\nL487: \nL488: `execute()`standard, with N_{inv}\nL489: \nL490: `stage_1_execute()``stage_2_execute()`staged, two passes\nL491: \nL492:   * 04\nL493: \nL494: Pointwise operations\nL495: \nL496: Element-wise arithmetic on equal-length vectors in the NTT domain. Both operands must already be in Montgomery form.\nL591: ### Writing Kernels\nL592: \nL593: In coefficient form, the product c of polynomials a and b requires a standard convolution: two forward transforms, a pointwise multiplication, and one inverse transform.\nL594: \nL595: A = fwd-NTT(a), B = fwd-NTT(b), C = A ⊙ B, c = inv-NTT(C)\nL596: \nL597: (where ⊙ is pointwise multiplication on equal-length vectors in the NTT domain, in Montgomery form)\nL598: \nL599: 【18†Image: Pipeline: a and b to A-hat and B-hat by forward NTT, C-hat equals A-hat pointwise times B-hat, inverse NTT to c】\nL600: With cuPQC-NTT, this entire pipeline runs as a single kernel in this example. Intermediate transforms never leave shared memory. The only global memory traffic is the initial load at the start and the final store at the end, drastically reducing memory bandwidth bottlenecks. The twiddle tables, Montgomery constants, and N^{−1} are built once outside this kernel and reused for the batch.\nL601: \nL602:     `using namespace cupqc;\nL603:     using NTT  = decltype(Algorithm<algorithm::NTT>() + Direction<nttDirection::FORWARD>()\nL604:                           + Precision<uint32_t>() + Size<1024>() + Block() + BlockDim<128>());\nL605:     using INTT = decltype(Algorithm<algorithm::NTT>() + Direction<nttDirection::INVERSE>()\nL606:                           + Precision<uint32_t>() + Size<1024>() + Block() + BlockDim<128>());\nL607: \nL608:     // c = inv-NTT( fwd-NTT(a) ⊙ fwd-NTT(b) ), one block per polynomial\nL609:     __global__ void convolution(uint32_t* c, const uint32_t* a, const uint32_t* b,\nL610:                                 const uint32_t* twiddles, const uint32_t* inv_twiddles,\nL611:                                 const nttConst<uint32_t> ntt_const, const uint32_t N_inv)\nL612:     {\nL613:        constexpr uint32_t N = NTT::Size;\nL614:        __shared__ uint32_t a_hat[N];\nL615:        __shared__ uint32_t b_hat[N];\nL616: \nL617:        NTT().load_to_mont(a_hat, a + blockIdx.x * N, ntt_const);\nL618:        NTT().load_to_mont(b_hat, b + blockIdx.x * N, ntt_const);\nL619:        __syncthreads();\nL620:        NTT().execute(a_hat, twiddles, ntt_const.p);          // A_hat\nL621:        NTT().execute(b_hat, twiddles, ntt_const.p);          // B_hat\nL622:        __syncthreads();\nL623:        NTT().mul(a_hat, b_hat, ntt_const.p);                 // C_hat = A_hat ⊙ B_hat\nL624:        __syncthreads();\nL625:        INTT().execute(a_hat, inv_twiddles, ntt_const.p, N_inv);\nL626:        __syncthreads();\nL627:        INTT().store_from_mont(a_hat, c + blockIdx.x * N, ntt_const);\nL628:     }\nL629:     `\nL630: ### Performance\nL631: \nL632: Test Environment\nL633:   * CPU Baseline: Intel Xeon Gold 5512U (28 cores, 56 logical CPUs).\nL634:   * GPU: NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada Generation.\nL635:   * Metrics: Wall time in seconds (measuring the fastest run rather than a mean).\nL636:   * Batching: Single-kernel sizes through 2^{12} use batch 100. Staged sizes from 2^{14} use batch 1. On the time chart, CPU uses the same batch at each N.\nL637:   * NT: Threads per block, set with `BlockDim`. These runs use 128, 256, 512, and 1024. Each GPU time point is the fastest measured configuration at that N: minimum over NT, and over `SubSize` for staged.\nL638: 32-bit NTT Time\nL639: \nL640: Wall time versus transform size for 32-bit coefficients; lower is faster. The break between 2^{12} and 2^{14} is the switch from a single kernel at batch 100 to staged transforms at batch 1, which is why the CPU times drop even though N increased. The GPU is fastest at every size. On the small single-kernel points, the 28-core CPU is slower than one core: there is not enough work to amortize the parallel launch.\nL641: \nL642: CPU VS GPU\nL643: \nL644: 32-bit NTT\nL645: \nL646: TIME · SECONDS, LOG SCALE\nL647: 1 CPU core 28-core CPU GPU\nL648: \nL649: SINGLE KERNEL · BATCH 100 STAGED · BATCH 1\nL650: \nL651: GPU points use the fastest NT, and SubSize for staged, at each N.\nL652: \nL653: The same 32-bit comparison as a multiple, from 2^{8}. Each pair of columns is GPU versus the 28-core CPU and versus one core. The chips under a size are the GPU NT, and `SubSize` for staged, for that bar. Single-kernel columns use the batch among 1, 10, and 100 with the largest 28-core speedup (batch 100 on every size shown); staged columns stay at batch 1.\nL654: \nL655: VS CPU\nL656: 32-bit NTT\nL657: \nL658: SPEEDUP · TIMES FASTER\nL659: \nL660: vs 28-core CPU vs 1 CPU core\nL661: \nL662: SINGLE KERNEL · BEST BATCH STAGED · BATCH 1\nL663: \nL664: 64-bit NTT Time\nL665: \nL666: Same wall-time axes as the 32-bit chart, GPU only, one line per NT. A staged point is the fastest `SubSize` for that NT, not the global winner. On the staged side the lines sit within a few percent of each other; the dashes are only so you can tell them apart.\nL667: \nL668: BY NT\nL669: \nL670: 64-bit NTT\nL671: \nL672: TIME · SECONDS, LOG SCALE\nL673: \nL674: NT 128 NT 256 NT 512 NT 1024\nL675: \nL676: SINGLE KERNEL · BATCH 100 STAGED · BATCH 1\nL677: Use the grid to choose NT from transform size. The circled cell is the fastest NT at that N; every other cell is the penalty for a different choice.\nL678: \nL679: NT BY SIZE\nL680: \nL681: 64-bit NTT\nL682: \nL683: PENALTY · % SLOWER THAN FASTEST NT\nL684: \nL685: fastest fasterslower\nL686: \nL687: SINGLE KERNEL · BATCH 100 STAGED · BATCH 1\nL688:   * Through 2^{12}: NT 256 is fastest at every measured single-kernel size. NT 128 is the miss, up to 44% slower at 2^{12}.\nL689:   * Staged sizes: The fastest NT depends on N; use the circled cell. NT 1024 is never the fastest pick and costs up to 56% at 2^{18}.\nL690:   * 2^{22} and above: Any of the four NTs is within 10% of the fastest.\nL691: \nL692: 32-bit vs 64-bit NTT Time\nL693: Same GPU wall-time series, 32-bit and 64-bit coefficients on one plot. Each point is the fastest NT, and `SubSize` for staged, at that N. The 64-bit line is slower at every size, from 1.1× at 2^{7} to 1.9× at 2^{24}.\nL694: \nL695: 32 VS 64\nL696: \nL697: GPU NTT\nL698: \nL699: TIME · SECONDS, LOG SCALE\nL700: \nL701: 32-bit 64-bit\nL702: \nL703: SINGLE KERNEL · BATCH 100 STAGED · BATCH 1\nL704: ## BigInt and NTT Takeaways\nL705: \nL706: Four configuration choices account for the largest swings in the results above:\nL707:   1. cuPQC-BigInt execution model: `Thread()` with TPI 1 delivers the highest throughput at 256 and 512 bits. At 1024 bits and above, `Warp()` takes the lead.\nL708:   2. cuPQC-BigInt Barrett reduction: At 1024 bits and above, Barrett reduction achieves 2.6× to 2.9× the throughput of reduction by division. At 256 bits the precomputation does not pay for itself, and `mul_mod` remains the better choice.\nL709:   3. cuPQC-NTT threads per block: The optimal NT varies with N. On 64-bit staged transforms, NT 1024 carries a penalty of up to 56% at 2^{18}.\nL710:   4. cuPQC-NTT `SubSize`: On staged transforms, the choice of `SubSize` shifts the runtime of a 2^{20} transform by roughly 20% across its valid values.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquality-factored sum-check - Akita Book (https://layerzero-labs.github.io/akita/foundations/eq-factored-sumcheck.html)\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://layerzero-labs.github.io/akita/foundations/eq-factored-sumcheck.html\",\"pattern\":\"mixed\"}); Total lines: 107\nL87: Instances that share the same factor can also share the specialized round logic. A mixed batch with different equality factors cannot omit the same inner coefficient under one common round equation. Such a batch uses ordinary compressed sum-check messages instead. The prover may still use split equality tables internally to save memory.\n"